That's arguably an implementation bug in the Unix
versions of NetHack, though.

As a renmant of ancient times, the _only_ use for
wizard mode was indeed, debugging Hack/NetHack (and
the rest of the installed games) by user "wizard",
the assigned Unix User ID for the owner of the
system's games (I used to _be_ "wizard" at a BSD 4.2
& 4.3 University site, a couple of decades ago).  At
that point it made sense that only that one specific
account could use "wizard mode". Locking other
players out of wizard mode both prevented mischief
on multi-user systems with untrusted users, and kept
the players (involuntarily) unspoiled.

That is no longer the situation, and NetHack should
pay attention to the name in the "-u" flag, not to
the login account name, in deciding whether to enter
wizard mode or to fall back to discovery mode.

On an MS-Windows system, despite the relatively
recent addition of user accounts and account names,
that same brokenness does not occur, and "nethack -u
wizard -D" indeed puts the user in "wizard mode",
regardless of the user's login name, as expected.
Wizard mode today is used for a more powerful way to
explore the game, and not (as much) for debugging at
all.

Moreover, arguably the "-u wizard" is now also
redundant, and merely the "-D" flag should suffice
in all environments. It is certainly an unambiguous
enough signal of intent.

